          paragraphs: [
            "<strong>Oxytocin for PPH</strong>: 20 IU is two 10 IU/mL ampoules (2 mL) added to 1 L of Ringer's lactate — label the bag 'oxytocin 20 IU in 1,000 mL, [time], [rate]' — and run at 60 drops a minute with a 20 drops/mL set (= 180 mL/h; 40 drops/min with a 15 drops/mL set is 160 mL/h), 'fast' meaning the bag is in over about 5–6 hours, not wide open; if 40 IU in 1 L is prescribed over 4 hours, that is 250 mL/h = 83 drops/min with a 20 drops/mL set (and the woman is watched for fluid overload). <strong>Oxytocin for induction</strong>: 5 IU (0.5 mL) in 500 mL = 10 mU/mL; a rate of 1 mU/min is 0.1 mL/min = 6 mL/h on a pump (2 drops/min with a 20 drops/mL set — too slow to count reliably, which is why pumps or a 2.5 IU dilution are used); 2 mU/min = 12 mL/h; 4 = 24; 8 = 48; 16 = 96; 32 mU/min = 192 mL/h. With 2.5 IU in 500 mL (5 mU/mL): 2 mU/min = 24 mL/h = 8 drops/min with a 20 drops/mL set — the usual starting rate in units without pumps, doubled every 30 minutes. <strong>The rate is written on the partograph with each change, and the person who increases it counts the contractions first.</strong>",
            "<strong>Magnesium sulphate from 50% ampoules</strong> (50% means 50 g in 100 mL = 0.5 g/mL = 5 g in a 10 mL ampoule, 1 g in 2 mL): the <strong>4 g IV loading dose is 8 mL of 50%</strong>, diluted to 20 mL (12 mL of water for injection or saline) to make a 20% solution, given over at least 5 minutes — one millilitre every 15 seconds is a safe pace; the <strong>10 g IM loading dose is two injections of 5 g = 10 mL of 50% each</strong>, each with 1 mL of 2% lignocaine drawn into the same syringe, deep into the upper outer quadrant of each buttock; the <strong>5 g maintenance dose is 10 mL of 50%</strong> plus lignocaine, alternating buttocks, every 4 hours; the <strong>2 g dose for a recurrent fit is 4 mL of 50%</strong> diluted to 10 mL; and the <strong>Zuspan infusion of 1 g/hour</strong> is, for example, 10 g (20 mL of 50%) in 500 mL of saline (= 1 g per 52 mL) run at 52 mL/h, or 20 g in 1 L at 50 mL/h — <strong>by pump</strong>, because a free-running magnesium infusion that is accidentally opened delivers a lethal dose in minutes, and where there is no pump the IM regimen is used. The woman transferred from a health centre after her loading dose has the <strong>time of the IM dose written on her referral note</strong>, because her next 5 g is due four hours later, wherever she is.",
            "<strong>Hydralazine</strong>: a 20 mg ampoule (powder or solution) diluted to 20 mL with water for injection gives 1 mg/mL; <strong>5 mg = 5 mL</strong>, slow IV over 5 minutes, blood pressure every 5 minutes; a 10 mg dose is 10 mL. <strong>Labetalol IV</strong> 5 mg/mL: 20 mg = 4 mL over 2 minutes. <strong>Terbutaline</strong> 0.5 mg/mL: 250 µg = <strong>0.5 mL</strong> subcutaneously. <strong>Calcium gluconate 10%</strong>: 10 mL = 1 g, slow IV over 3–10 minutes — <strong>drawn up and kept next to the magnesium</strong>. <strong>Ergometrine</strong> 0.5 mg/mL: 0.2 mg = <strong>0.4 mL</strong> — not the whole ampoule. <strong>Tranexamic acid</strong> 500 mg/5 mL: 1 g = two ampoules = 10 mL, over 10 minutes (1 mL/min). <strong>Gentamicin</strong> 40 mg/mL (adult) or 10 mg/mL (paediatric): for a 3 kg newborn at 5 mg/kg = 15 mg = <strong>1.5 mL of the 10 mg/mL strength</strong>, or 0.375 mL of the 40 mg/mL strength — which is why the paediatric strength or a dilution is used. <strong>Ampicillin</strong> 500 mg vial reconstituted to 5 mL (100 mg/mL): a 3 kg baby at 50 mg/kg = 150 mg = <strong>1.5 mL</strong>. <strong>Nevirapine</strong> 10 mg/mL: a 3.2 kg baby gets 1.5 mL (15 mg) daily. <strong>Phenobarbital</strong> 200 mg/mL (adult ampoule) for a 3 kg baby at 20 mg/kg = 60 mg = <strong>0.3 mL</strong> — dilute 1 mL to 10 mL (20 mg/mL) and give 3 mL, because 0.3 mL of a concentrated ampoule drawn into a 2 mL syringe is a guess.",
          ],
